WEBVTT

00:02.400 --> 00:05.040
There's an experiment teachers use to show why instructions matter.

00:05.740 --> 00:08.260
They ask kids to write out how to make a peanut butter and jelly sandwich.

00:08.820 --> 00:13.200
Then the teacher chooses a student and follows their instructions quite literally.

00:14.160 --> 00:17.900
Bread stays in the bag, jelly stays in the jar, or maybe it gets smeared all over the

00:17.900 --> 00:18.140
table.

00:18.700 --> 00:19.500
It's pure chaos.

00:20.320 --> 00:22.940
That's exactly what happens when you give AI a vague prompt.

00:23.700 --> 00:24.240
That isn't dumb.

00:25.080 --> 00:25.440
You were vague.

00:26.100 --> 00:28.620
Clarity compounds confusion is expensive.

00:34.200 --> 00:36.940
Here's the test I use for every project before I touch keyboard.

00:37.880 --> 00:43.000
Write out the outcome in one sentence, not a paragraph, not a brainstorm, just one sentence.

00:43.660 --> 00:50.100
If you can't stop, because no model, no team, no system will save you from the fuzziest

00:50.100 --> 00:50.800
idea ever.

00:55.780 --> 00:57.140
Unpopular opinion here.

00:57.800 --> 01:00.900
Most prompt engineering courses are selling you a skill that doesn't even exist.

01:01.560 --> 01:07.040
Since the AI boom, there's been a wave of self-proclaimed AI gurus charging hundreds for

01:07.040 --> 01:10.840
prompt tricks, or prompt bags, or a prompt library.

01:11.660 --> 01:12.240
But here's the thing.

01:12.700 --> 01:13.660
The skill isn't prompting.

01:14.120 --> 01:14.920
The skill is thinking.

01:15.800 --> 01:19.300
And thinking has and always will be free.

01:19.880 --> 01:20.860
Don't pay for prompting.

01:24.440 --> 01:25.280
Trust me.

01:25.440 --> 01:30.360
I used to buy every AI course or consume all of the free materials, every prompt pack,

01:30.660 --> 01:31.800
every secret framework.

01:32.520 --> 01:35.560
Every one of them made me a better prompter and a worse thinker.

01:36.300 --> 01:41.260
The moment I stopped buying courses and started actually defining outcomes, everything changed.

01:46.260 --> 01:48.320
Every entrepreneur I know is trying to scale.

01:49.280 --> 01:52.300
Almost none of them can tell you the difference between scaling and stretching.

01:53.340 --> 01:57.560
If your systems break the moment you double your output, you weren't built to scale.

01:58.280 --> 02:00.700
You were being stretched thin and pretending you can scale.

02:01.420 --> 02:02.220
Real scale is quiet.

02:03.200 --> 02:04.400
It doesn't feel like a hustle.

02:04.820 --> 02:05.920
It just feels like math.

02:11.180 --> 02:13.480
Growth without structure isn't growth.

02:14.560 --> 02:15.680
It's decay at high speed.

02:16.400 --> 02:20.280
I watched a founder 3x her revenue in a year and lose her entire team.

02:20.980 --> 02:23.620
More money, more chaos, zero progress.

02:24.360 --> 02:26.620
Structure is a boring thing that makes freedom possible.

02:27.400 --> 02:28.560
That makes freedom possible.

02:34.120 --> 02:39.800
A federal judge ruled that using consumer chat GPT for legal research can actually waive

02:39.800 --> 02:41.060
attorney-client privilege.

02:41.840 --> 02:44.480
69% of lawyers are using these tools right now.

02:45.780 --> 02:48.140
Most of them have never even read the terms of service.

02:48.740 --> 02:49.900
The model is logging a queries.

02:50.660 --> 02:54.320
Yes, chat GPT is storing them, training on them, and that's not privilege.

02:54.980 --> 02:55.960
That's public knowledge.

02:56.720 --> 02:57.340
That's risk.

03:01.620 --> 03:04.200
Here's what almost nobody tells you about AI automation.

03:05.120 --> 03:06.400
The bottleneck is never the tools.

03:06.940 --> 03:07.700
It's never the prompt.

03:08.180 --> 03:11.840
It's almost always the thinking that happens before you even touch the keyboard.

03:16.840 --> 03:21.480
The best way you can think about AI is to treat it like an extremely high IQ child.

03:22.020 --> 03:23.700
It can do things that you can't.

03:24.280 --> 03:26.460
It processes information in ways that you can't.

03:26.460 --> 03:31.780
But you still have to give it a role, a brand, a tone, a sequence.

03:32.680 --> 03:36.120
If you leave out any of those, you'll get exactly what you'd expect.

03:36.800 --> 03:42.100
A genius with no context, doing something completely wrong very fast.

03:47.520 --> 03:48.460
Clarity compounds.

03:48.840 --> 03:49.580
Confusion is expensive.

03:50.500 --> 03:54.800
Every menu you spend writing vague prompts is a minute you could have spent writing one

03:54.800 --> 03:55.560
clear sentence.

03:56.400 --> 03:58.440
The one sentence changes the whole output.

03:59.100 --> 04:00.800
That's the leverage point most people miss.

04:06.340 --> 04:08.540
Most people are stuck doing basic prompting.

04:09.120 --> 04:11.400
Very few people build actual projects.

04:11.920 --> 04:13.340
Projects are context libraries.

04:14.140 --> 04:18.780
They're role definitions, brand guides, processing templates, example outputs.

04:18.780 --> 04:22.000
There are things that you use every single time.

04:22.340 --> 04:24.900
Prompting is primitive, but projects are powerful.

04:25.600 --> 04:31.140
The moment I switch that thinking up, my output quality multiplied, my time per task collapsed.

04:36.320 --> 04:38.660
Here's what most people get wrong about AI.

04:39.420 --> 04:41.080
They think it'll just write for them.

04:41.380 --> 04:42.360
Well, it won't.

04:42.600 --> 04:44.680
It'll multiply whatever you bring to it.

04:44.680 --> 04:45.880
Bring confusion.

04:46.460 --> 04:47.240
It amplifies confusion.

04:47.900 --> 04:48.820
Bring a clear voice.

04:49.540 --> 04:50.560
It amplifies your voice.

04:51.600 --> 04:54.500
AI is just a mirror with a megaphone.

04:58.400 --> 05:00.460
Ten percent more efficient on your prompt.

05:01.320 --> 05:02.440
Five times better output.

05:03.240 --> 05:05.080
That's the math that almost nobody tells you.

05:05.480 --> 05:06.620
One crisp sentence.

05:07.380 --> 05:08.620
One defined role.

05:09.540 --> 05:10.280
One clear outcome.

05:10.280 --> 05:15.940
And the same model that was mediocre yesterday suddenly feels like a different tool.

05:16.660 --> 05:18.900
The leverage is in the effort that most people skip.

05:23.100 --> 05:26.040
I used to think that freedom at walking away from your work.

05:26.600 --> 05:30.840
Turns out freedom is the work that you actually want to do, that you choose to do.

05:31.460 --> 05:32.720
Built the way you want it.

05:33.300 --> 05:34.960
Delivered at the pace you can actually sustain.

05:36.080 --> 05:36.840
That's not a dream.

05:36.840 --> 05:38.860
That's actually a design problem.

05:39.240 --> 05:42.740
And design problems, those, they can actually be solved.

